Derive dynamic balance and relaxation for a reversible model (@chemistry-reversible-model: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "../general-chemistry/equilibrium/Reaction-quotient-and-equilibrium-constant.lingua#r_2E5AXFZWT12G4Y2BGG89YHSG4G") { r_08C68F9BE198AA15DE975BAF90
Consider a stipulated benign symbolic A ⇌ B conversion in a closed, well-mixed, fixed-volume model at fixed temperature. Assume equal conserved constituent units, no other reactions, and elementary ideal first-order directional fluxes j_f=k_f a and j_r=k_r b. Here a and b are concentrations, C=a+b is constant, k_f,k_r>0 have inverse-time units, and fluxes have concentration/time units. This is a teaching model, not evidence identifying a real reaction mechanism.

The species account is b′=k_f(C−b)−k_r b. Let λ=k_f+k_r and b_eq=Ck_f/λ. Rearranging gives b′=−λ(b−b_eq). The integrating-factor argument from [Connect a rate model with an initial value and an admissible trajectory](Connect-a-rate-model-with-an-initial-value-and-an-admissible-trajectory.lingua#r_01743BDDD7178F3F01AE693646) yields b(t)=b_eq+(b₀−b_eq)exp(−λt). Substitution checks both derivative and initial value. For t≥0, the exponential factor lies in (0,1], so b(t) is a convex combination of b₀ and b_eq. Thus 0≤b₀≤C implies 0≤b(t)≤C, and a=C−b conserves the total. Uniqueness follows by applying the same factor to the difference of two solutions.

At b_eq, both directional fluxes equal Ck_fk_r/λ. They need not vanish although their difference does. Under this particular ideal elementary model, the equilibrium activity ratio b/a is k_f/k_r. With dimensionless activities a_A=a/c_ref and a_B=b/c_ref using the same reference and ideal approximation, Q=a_B/a_A=b/a. K denotes its equilibrium value at the stated temperature and conventions. This does not make a ratio of arbitrary fitted overall rate constants a universal thermodynamic equilibrium constant. The general shared [Distinguish the present composition from the equilibrium relation](../general-chemistry/equilibrium/Reaction-quotient-and-equilibrium-constant.lingua#r_2E5AXFZWT12G4Y2BGG89YHSG4G) treatment retains activities and standard states.

For C=1 mmol/L, b₀=0, k_f=3/4 min⁻¹ and k_r=1/4 min⁻¹, λ=1 min⁻¹, b_eq=3/4 mmol/L and K=3. At t=ln2 min, b=3/8 and a=5/8 mmol/L. At t=2 ln2 min, b=9/16 and a=7/16. The half time measures halving the deviation from equilibrium; it does not generally mean halving the total reactant concentration. Starting above b_eq reverses the net direction and approaches the same equilibrium.

Multiplying both rate constants by a positive factor α leaves K and equilibrium composition unchanged but divides every relaxation time by α. This illustrates an equilibrium-preserving acceleration, not a universal microscopic catalyst mechanism. A real catalyst may create additional intermediates and change the observed rate law; the complete same-system equilibrium constraints remain. If one rate constant is zero, the model approaches the corresponding boundary composition. If both vanish, every initial composition is stationary and there is no unique rate-selected equilibrium. Do not call a kinetically frozen mixture thermodynamic equilibrium merely because it stops changing.

A different open account can also be stationary. At a=b=1/2 with the original constants, the net reaction produces B at 1/4 mmol L⁻¹ min⁻¹. An external account adding A and removing B at exactly that rate holds the concentrations fixed despite unequal reaction fluxes. That driven stationary state has Q=1, not K=3. This is a mathematical boundary comparison, not a process design; energy and other transport requirements would need their own account.

**Read and compare:** [Chemistry 2e — Flowers, Theopold, Langley, and Robinson](../general-chemistry/sources/Chemistry-OpenStax.lingua#r_02Y5N2VMTTPBDSNYPD4Z26EZ94), 13.1–13.3, then [Distinguish the present composition from the equilibrium relation](../general-chemistry/equilibrium/Reaction-quotient-and-equilibrium-constant.lingua#r_2E5AXFZWT12G4Y2BGG89YHSG4G) and [Relate catalysis to pathways, selectivity, and equilibrium constraints](../general-chemistry/kinetics/Catalysis-and-competing-pathways.lingua#r_4Z85MENW43NS707PTCHNTDTDME). Derive the closed-model solution, conserved total, admissible interval and direction from each side. Explain what extra evidence distinguishes a closed equilibrium, a frozen composition and a driven stationary observation. Keep physical equilibrium, kinetic assumptions and measurement evidence separate.

**Continue:** [Distinguish the present composition from the equilibrium relation](../general-chemistry/equilibrium/Reaction-quotient-and-equilibrium-constant.lingua#r_2E5AXFZWT12G4Y2BGG89YHSG4G).

